UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 7, Lesson 11: Improvisation and acting skills', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Appreciating a peer scene means you name one acting choice and the effect it had on you as the audience. A choice might be a pause, a still body, a short sentence, an entrance that waited in the doorway, or a voice that stayed at speaking level. The effect is what that choice made you understand, such as nervousness, urgency, or care. Kind feedback talks about the work, not about the person''s body, clothes, or worth. ''When you paused before the title, I believed Sam was nervous, because the librarian had to wait'' is useful. ''That was good'' is kind but too vague to help. ''You are bad at drama'' is neither kind nor specific. Give one notice, not a list of faults. The peer should be able to repeat the choice on purpose next time.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same arts idea to a new artwork or performance, then compare two choices for clarity of effect.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nA peer stood completely still while the other student searched the trolley for the blue nature guide, and did not add a joke. Which audience note is kind and specific about that acting choice?\n\nFEEDBACK\nYour stillness made the search feel serious, because you did not fidget or break the moment with a joke. The choice is the still body. The effect is that the search felt serious to the audience. The note talks about the work, so the actor can choose stillness again on purpose. ''That was really good'' does not say what to keep, and a comment about someone''s face or clothes is not about the acting.\n\nWATCH FOR\nSaying only that a scene was good, or that a person is bad at drama, never names a choice or its effect. Empty praise feels kind and leaves the actor with nothing to repeat. A put-down feels honest and teaches nothing about the scene. Repair both by pointing to one thing the actor did and one thing you understood because of it.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nName one acting choice in the late-arrival scene and the effect that choice has on the audience, in two kind sentences. Scene, original and not the library: Jo enters late to a rehearsal, stops in the doorway, and sits on the floor at the edge rather than in the spare chair. The other student keeps reading aloud and does not look up until the last line. Sentence one names a choice. Sentence two names its effect. Do not score anyone, and do not comment on bodies, clothes, or worth.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one artistic choice you changed and why. Movement or instrument work is optional, adult-supervised and can be described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Drama","learning_intention":"Appreciating a peer scene means you name one acting choice and the effect it had on you as the audience. A choice might be a pause, a still body, a short sentence, an entrance that waited in the doorway, or a voice that stayed at speaking level. The effect is what that choice made you understand, such as nervousness, urgency, or care. Kind feedback talks about the work, not about the person''s body, clothes, or worth. ''When you paused before the title, I believed Sam was nervous, because the librarian had to wait'' is useful. ''That was good'' is kind but too vague to help. ''You are bad at drama'' is neither kind nor specific. Give one notice, not a list of faults. The peer should be able to repeat the choice on purpose next time.","lesson_number":11,"lesson_role":"Learn","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: scrap paper, crayons, safe classroom instruments or clear movement space with an adult nearby"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["DR4-MAK-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Name one acting choice in the late-arrival scene and the effect that choice has on the audience, in two kind sentences. Scene, original and not the library: Jo enters late to a rehearsal, stops in the doorway, and sits o Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using feedback on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Saying only that a scene was good, or that a person is bad at drama, never names a choice or its effect.. Correction: Your stillness made the search feel serious, because you did not fidget or break the moment with a joke.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Saying only that a scene was good, or that a person is bad at drama, never names a choice or its effect. Empty praise feels kind and leaves the actor with nothing to repeat. A put-down feels honest and teaches nothing about the scene. Repair both by pointing to one thing the actor did and one thing you understood because of it. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating feedback on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with feedback, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about feedback with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for feedback. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for feedback. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work","Your stillness made the search feel serious, because you did not fidget or break the moment with a joke","Saying only that a scene was good, or that a person is bad at drama, never names a choice or its effect","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named"],"question":"A peer stood completely still while the other student searched the trolley for the blue nature guide, and did not add a joke. Which audience note is kind and specific about that acting choice?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for feedback; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Your stillness made the search feel serious, because you did not fidget or break the moment with a joke","Careful use of choice with clear evidence","Saying only that a scene was good, or that a person is bad at drama, never names a choice or its effect","Appreciating a peer scene means you name one acting choice and the effect it had on you as the audience"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with feedback?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named choice, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Saying only that a scene was good, or that a person is bad at drama, never names a choice or its effect","Name choice only, without linking it to feedback","Apply feedback to new material and name the choice used"],"question":"Which response best applies feedback with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses elements, techniques and audience effect appropriately for this lesson on feedback. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Saying only that a scene was good, or that a person is bad at drama, never names a choice or its effect","Your stillness made the search feel serious, because you did not fidget or break the moment with a joke","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance"],"question":"Which response best evaluates elements, techniques and audience effect when analysing feedback?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name a specific element or technique and say what effect it creates for feedback","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance","Saying only that a scene was good, or that a person is bad at drama, never names a choice or its effect"],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about feedback?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea (Appreciating a peer scene means you name one acting choice and the) with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Say it looks nice with no element or technique named","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance","Naming the pause before the book title, and the wait it caused, is specific and kind feedback on the library scene","Saying only that a scene was good, or that a person is bad at drama, never names a choice or its effect"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about feedback?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name the artistic choice, its effect, and keep cultural protocols respectful","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster","Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue"],"question":"Which action keeps elements, techniques and audience effect accurate for feedback?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name choice only, with no linked example","Name one acting choice in the late-arrival scene and the effect that choice has on the audience, in two kind sentences","Retype the worked example with no new example","Saying only that a scene was good, or that a person is bad at drama, never names a choice or its effect"],"question":"Which independent response best applies feedback to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about feedback with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating feedback on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using feedback on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for feedback. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with feedback, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Saying only that a scene was good, or that a person is bad at drama, never names a choice or its effect.. Correction: Your stillness made the search feel serious, because you did not fidget or break the moment with a joke.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent artwork or performance notes and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s5-drama-y9-w06-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 4 alignment to Drama 7–10 (2023) outcome DR4-MAK-01. Content-point mapping pending. Not NESA verified. Scenes are original. Physical work is optional, consented, and adult-supervised.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S4","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise feedback.","Read the mission before you start making or performing.","Keep the audience effect of your choice in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not name an effect clearly."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the element or technique that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore elements, safety or respectful practice.","Prefer a named artistic reason over a guess that merely looks colourful."],"submit":["Confirm that your choice and reason are both present.","Proofread element names and safety notes.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact element, technique or cue before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key artistic choices.","Say what each choice does for the artwork or audience.","A final praise word alone is weaker than a named effect."],"teach2":["Compare a weak choice with a stronger one.","Change the element or technique, not only the decoration.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh subject or piece that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original work notes and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill on new material."]},"subject":"Drama","success_criteria":["I can use feedback with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify choice and explain why it matters.","I can practise effect independently on a new prompt.","I can notice kindness needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Appreciating a peer scene means you name one acting choice and the effect it had on you as the audience.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no artistic reason","Appreciating a peer scene means you name one acting choice and the effect it had on you as the audience","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about feedback?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nNaming the pause before the book title, and the wait it caused, is specific and kind feedback on the library scene. Peer choice: Sam stops for a full breath before saying ''blue nature guide,'' and the librarian does not speak over the gap. Feedback: ''When you paused before the title, I believed Sam was nervous, because the librarian had to wait with you.'' That names the choice (the pause) and the effect (nervousness, shown by the wait). A second note on a different choice stays just as exact: ''Your still hands made the apology feel serious, because nothing fidgeted the moment away.''\n\nTRY FIRST\nA peer stood completely still while the other student searched the trolley for the blue nature guide, and did not add a joke. Which audience note is kind and specific about that acting choice?\n\nFEEDBACK\nYour stillness made the search feel serious, because you did not fidget or break the moment with a joke. The choice is the still body. The effect is that the search felt serious to the audience. The note talks about the work, so the actor can choose stillness again on purpose. ''That was really good'' does not say what to keep, and a comment about someone''s face or clothes is not about the acting.","explain":"Appreciating a peer scene means you name one acting choice and the effect it had on you as the audience. A choice might be a pause, a still body, a short sentence, an entrance that waited in the doorway, or a voice that stayed at speaking level. The effect is what that choice made you understand, such as nervousness, urgency, or care. Kind feedback talks about the work, not about the person''s body, clothes, or worth. ''When you paused before the title, I believed Sam was nervous, because the librarian had to wait'' is useful. ''That was good'' is kind but too vague to help. ''You are bad at drama'' is neither kind nor specific. Give one notice, not a list of faults. The peer should be able to repeat the choice on purpose next time.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Saying only that a scene was good, or that a person is bad at drama, never names a choice or its effect. Empty praise feels kind and leaves the actor with nothing to repeat. A put-down feels honest and teaches nothing about the scene. Repair both by pointing to one thing the actor did and one thing you understood because of it. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Feedback that names a pause and its effect, beside praise that names nothing.","caption":"Name one choice and the effect it had.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Specific note</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">The pause showed nerves</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Vague note</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">That was really good</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Naming the pause before the book title, and the wait it caused, is specific and kind feedback on the library scene. Peer Then write your own artistic reason.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.\nJuno: Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Naming the pause before the book title, and the wait it caused, is specific and kind feedback on the library scene. Peer Then write your own artistic reason."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no artistic reason"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about feedback?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nNaming the pause before the book title, and the wait it caused, is specific and kind feedback on the library scene. Peer choice: Sam stops for a full breath before saying ''blue nature guide,'' and the librarian does not speak over the gap. Feedback: ''When you paused before the title, I believed Sam was nervous, because the librarian had to wait with you.'' That names the choice (the pause) and the effect (nervousness, shown by the wait). A second note on a different choice stays just as exact: ''Your still hands made the apology feel serious, because nothing fidgeted the moment away.'' Look at each line, sound, movement or role choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key artistic choices from the example and say what each one does for the audience or artwork.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful response names the element or technique and its effect, not only “it looks nice”.","explain":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final praise word without naming a choice does not show arts thinking. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Show the artistic choice","visual":[{"alt":"A note about an acting choice contrasted with a comment about the person.","caption":"Talk about the choice, kindly and precisely.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">About the work</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Stillness made it serious</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">About the person</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">You are bad at drama</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A guess with no artistic reason","Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about feedback?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nSaying only that a scene was good, or that a person is bad at drama, never names a choice or its effect. Empty praise feels kind and leaves the actor with nothing to repeat. A put-down feels honest and teaches nothing about the scene. Repair both by pointing to one thing the actor did and one thing you understood because of it. Here is a more helpful example: Naming the pause before the book title, and the wait it caused, is specific and kind feedback on the library scene. Peer choice: Sam stops for a full breath before saying ''blue nature guide,'' and the librarian does not speak over the gap. Feedback: ''When you paused before the title, I believed Sam was nervous, because the librarian had to wait with you.'' That names the choice (the pause) and the effect (nervousness, shown by the wait). A second note on a different choice stays just as exact: ''Your still hands made the apology feel serious, because nothing fidgeted the moment away.''\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice weakens the work, then write a stronger choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nYour stillness made the search feel serious, because you did not fidget or break the moment with a joke. The choice is the still body. The effect is that the search felt serious to the audience. The note talks about the work, so the actor can choose stillness again on purpose. ''That was really good'' does not say what to keep, and a comment about someone''s face or clothes is not about the acting.","explain":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the decoration without changing the artistic idea does not fix the error. Saying only that a scene was good, or that a person is bad at drama, never names a choice or its effect. Empty praise feels kind and leaves the actor with nothing to repeat. A put-down feels honest and teaches nothing about the scene. Repair both by pointing to one thing the actor did and one thing you understood because of it. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A guess with no artistic reason","Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about feedback?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nAppreciating a peer scene means you name one acting choice and the effect it had on you as the audience. A choice might be a pause, a still body, a short sentence, an entrance that waited in the doorway, or a voice that stayed at speaking level. The effect is what that choice made you understand, such as nervousness, urgency, or care. Kind feedback talks about the work, not about the person''s body, clothes, or worth. ''When you paused before the title, I believed Sam was nervous, because the librarian had to wait'' is useful. ''That was good'' is kind but too vague to help. ''You are bad at drama'' is neither kind nor specific. Give one notice, not a list of faults. The peer should be able to repeat the choice on purpose next time. Now choose a different subject or piece to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same arts idea as the model, but with different content or material.","explain":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new artwork or performance. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Transfer to a new artwork or performance"}],"title":"Year 7, Lesson 11: Improvisation and acting skills","unit_number":1,"unit_title":"Improvisation and acting skills","week":9,"week_in_unit":6,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nNaming the pause before the book title, and the wait it caused, is specific and kind feedback on the library scene. Peer choice: Sam stops for a full breath before saying ''blue nature guide,'' and the librarian does not speak over the gap. Feedback: ''When you paused before the title, I believed Sam was nervous, because the librarian had to wait with you.'' That names the choice (the pause) and the effect (nervousness, shown by the wait). A second note on a different choice stays just as exact: ''Your still hands made the apology feel serious, because nothing fidgeted the moment away.''\n\nWrite what makes this example effective, naming the element, technique or performance choice and its effect.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Feedback that names a pause and its effect, beside praise that names nothing.","caption":"Name one choice and the effect it had.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Specific note</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">The pause showed nerves</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Vague note</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">That was really good</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"A note about an acting choice contrasted with a comment about the person.","caption":"Talk about the choice, kindly and precisely.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">About the work</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Stillness made it serious</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">About the person</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">You are bad at drama</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 7"}') WHERE seed_key='s5-drama-y9-w06-l1' AND family_id IS NULL;
